        <p class="profile-detail-region">Battle · Central &amp; East Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Mikatagahara</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">25 January 1573; in the Japanese calendar the 22nd day of the 12th month of the 3rd year of Genki</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">The plain of Mikatagahara, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamamatsu">Hamamatsu</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totomi-province">Tōtōmi Province</a>, in present-day Shizuoka Prefecture, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Takeda under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a>, who led 30,000 men south in October 1572 while Yamagata Masakage led a second force of 5,000 into eastern Mikawa; the Tokugawa under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>, with 8,000 men at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamamatsu">Hamamatsu</a> Castle plus 3,000 reinforcements from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>. The Kōyō Gunkan says Shingen outnumbered Ieyasu three to one.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Tokugawa and Oda force was almost totally destroyed and many of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a>'s retainers were killed. Ieyasu escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamamatsu">Hamamatsu</a> Castle with five men, and a night attack by his remaining men helped persuade Shingen to withdraw.</span></div>
        </div>
