    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> fought on the plain against the advice of his own generals and of the Oda officers sent to help him, although Shingen had wished to avoid conflict and save his forces for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>.</li><li>Tokugawa arquebusiers opened the battle, but Takeda cavalry rapidly overran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honda-tadakatsu">Honda Tadakatsu</a>'s segment on the Tokugawa right, and a second cavalry charge followed by the Takeda main body drove the Tokugawa army into disorderly retreat.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamamatsu">Hamamatsu</a> with only five men and ordered the gates left open and braziers lit; the Takeda vanguard took this for a trap and camped for the night.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamamatsu">Hamamatsu</a> Castle, he is said to have ordered the gates left open and braziers lit, while Sakai Tadatsugu beat a large war drum. The Takeda vanguard took it for a trap and stopped to make camp.</p></aside>
    </section>

