    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1571</span><span class="rf-text">Aoyama Tadamon, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a>, resists the Takeda invasion of Mikawa and is later killed by a gunshot</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1572</span><span class="rf-text">Shingen leads 30,000 men south from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kofu">Kōfu</a> into Tōtōmi while Yamagata Masakage leads 5,000 into eastern Mikawa, and they take Yoshida Castle and Futamata Castle</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1572</span><span class="rf-text">The Tokugawa vanguard meets Yamagata Masakage and Baba Nobuharu at the Battle of Hitokotosaka</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">January 1573</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> draws up his forces on the plain of Mikatagahara, and Takeda cavalry overrun the Tokugawa right and the Oda reinforcements</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">January 1573</span><span class="rf-text">A second Takeda cavalry charge and the Takeda main body drive the Tokugawa army into retreat, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> flees to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamamatsu">Hamamatsu</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">January 1573</span><span class="rf-text">That night about 100 Tokugawa foot soldiers and 16 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matchlock">matchlock</a> gunners attack the Takeda camp and Shingen decides to withdraw</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">February 1573</span><span class="rf-text">Shingen is fatally wounded at the Siege of Noda Castle</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">May 1573</span><span class="rf-text">Shingen dies</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-mikatagahara" data-v="7294086627" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The armies take their ground on the plain</b> Ieyasu drew up his forces in a line on the plain of Mikatagahara. Shingen arrayed his men in the fish-scale formation in four lines, with the vanguard first and Baba Nobuharu in the fourth.</li><li><b>The Takeda cavalry overruns the Tokugawa right</b> As snow began to fall the Tokugawa arquebusiers opened fire. Naitō Masatoyo's vanguard cavalry attacked all the same and overran Honda Tadakatsu's segment on the Tokugawa right.</li><li><b>The Oda reinforcements are routed</b> Takeda horsemen exploited the opening and quickly assaulted the Oda reinforcements, who were overrun and routed. Their officer Hirate Hirohide was killed, and Sakuma Nobumori and Takigawa Kazumasu fled the battle.</li><li><b>The Takeda horse charge the Tokugawa rear</b> Having exploited the opening and assaulted the Oda reinforcements, the Takeda horsemen went on to charge the Tokugawa rear, which put them behind the Tokugawa centre.</li><li><b>The attack on the Tokugawa left is thrown back</b> A similar attack on the Tokugawa left was thwarted. The Tokugawa units there refused to yield ground and shrugged off the advance of the Takeda right, which prevented an encirclement of the centre.</li><li><b>Shingen rests his vanguard and brings up fresh horse</b> Shingen withdrew his vanguard to rest. He brought forward a new set of horsemen from the main body of the army, under Takeda Katsuyori, Obata Masamori and Saegusa Moritomo.</li><li><b>A two-pronged charge and the main body break the line</b> The fresh horsemen led a two-pronged cavalry charge into the weakening Tokugawa line, closely followed by the main body. Their combined weight drove the already battered Tokugawa army into a disorderly retreat.</li><li><b>Ieyasu flees and his units are overrun</b> Convinced by his retainers that his life was too important, Ieyasu fled the field. Several of his retainers died as their units were encircled and overrun by the Takeda forces.</li><li><b>The Takeda vanguard stops short of Hamamatsu</b> Ieyasu returned to Hamamatsu with five men and ordered the gates left open and braziers lit. The Takeda vanguard under Baba Nobuharu and Yamagata Masakage took this for a trap and stopped to make camp for the night short of the town.</li><li><b>A night raid throws the Takeda vanguard into confusion</b> A band of about one hundred Tokugawa foot soldiers and 16 matchlock gunners under Ōkubo Tadayo and Amano Yasukage attacked the Takeda camp and threw the vanguard of the Takeda army into confusion.</li><li><b>Shingen decides to withdraw</b> Uncertain of the Tokugawa strength, and worried that reinforcements from Oda Nobunaga or Uesugi Kenshin were on their way, Shingen decided to withdraw his forces to his own territories.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga.jpg" alt="Oda Nobunaga" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Odanobunaga.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">狩野宗秀 (Kanō Sōshū, 1551 - 1601), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1571 Aoyama Tadamon, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>, resisted a Takeda invasion of Mikawa by way of Asuke Castle, building a series of palisades to thwart the early incursion. He later drove the Takeda back at Shinpukuji, north of Dōdo Castle, but was killed in a battle by a gunshot and succeeded by his son Aoyama Tadanari.</p><p>In October 1572 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a> made a larger move. He had concluded alliances with his rivals to the east, the Later Hōjō clan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawara">Odawara</a> and the Satomi clan of Awa, and had waited for the snow to close the northern mountain passes against his northern rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-kenshin">Uesugi Kenshin</a>. He then led 30,000 men south from his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kofu">Kōfu</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totomi-province">Tōtōmi Province</a>, while Yamagata Masakage led a second force of 5,000 into eastern Mikawa. They quickly captured Yoshida Castle and Futamata Castle.</p><p>Shingen was campaigning against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> and seeking a route from Kōfu to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. His intent was not to attack Ieyasu or to seize <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamamatsu">Hamamatsu</a>; he wished to avoid conflict if possible, to save his forces for Nobunaga and the march on Kyoto. Ieyasu was based at Hamamatsu Castle. In the same month he sent Naitō Nobunari, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honda-tadakatsu">Honda Tadakatsu</a> and Ōkubo Tadasuke to lead the vanguard, which met Takeda forces under Yamagata Masakage and Baba Nobuharu at the Battle of Hitokotosaka. Tadakatsu and Nobunari fought well and broke through the Takeda attempts to encircle them.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Shingen led 30,000 men into Tōtōmi, and Yamagata Masakage led a separate force of 5,000 into eastern Mikawa. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> was at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamamatsu">Hamamatsu</a> Castle with 8,000 men, plus 3,000 reinforcements received from his ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>. The Kōyō Gunkan, a record of the Takeda family's military exploits, says that Shingen outnumbered Ieyasu three to one.</p><p>According to the same record, Shingen organised his men in the gyorin, or fish-scale, formation, to entice his opponent to attack. Oyamada Nobushige was in the vanguard, followed by Naitō Masatoyo and Yamagata Masakage. The third line was commanded by Katsuyori and Obata Masamori, and Baba Nobuharu was in the fourth. Opposing him, Ieyasu had gathered his available forces and arrayed them in a line.</p><p>Ieyasu's decision to fight was not shared by his advisers. Sakuma Nobumori and Takigawa Kazumasu, who had been sent by Nobunaga, and his own generals Matsudaira Koretada and Ishikawa Kazumasa advised against it. Ieyasu nevertheless refused to let the Takeda pass through his territory unhindered and drew up his forces on a high plain called Mikatagahara, then just north of Hamamatsu. The historian Kawai Atsushi viewed the decision as a necessity rather than a gamble: the Mikawa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> clans were aggressive and proud, and if Ieyasu had refused to fight the invaders he would have lost their respect and weakened his control of the province.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/honda-tadakatsu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/honda-tadakatsu.jpg" alt="Honda Tadakatsu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/honda-tadakatsu">Honda Tadakatsu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait-Honda-Tadakatsu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At around four in the afternoon, as snow began to fall, Tokugawa arquebusiers accompanied by a number of peasant stone-throwers opened fire on the Takeda formation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> had expected his firearms to overcome Shingen's much larger force. The expectation was quickly dispelled: Naitō Masatoyo's vanguard cavalry attacked and rapidly overran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honda-tadakatsu">Honda Tadakatsu</a>'s segment on the Tokugawa right. Takeda horsemen exploited the opening, assaulted the Oda reinforcements and charged the Tokugawa rear. The Oda forces were routed, the officer Hirate Hirohide was killed, and Sakuma Nobumori and Takigawa Kazumasu fled. A similar attack on the left was thwarted, because the Tokugawa units there refused to yield ground, and an encirclement of the Tokugawa centre was prevented.</p><p>Shingen then withdrew his vanguard to rest and brought forward fresh horsemen from the main body, ordering Takeda Katsuyori, Obata Masamori and Saegusa Moritomo to lead a two-pronged cavalry charge into the weakening Tokugawa line. The foot-soldier-heavy Takeda main body followed closely, and their combined weight drove the battered Tokugawa army into a disorderly retreat.</p><p>Ieyasu ordered Ōkubo Tadayo to plant his golden fan standard on a hill and rally the troops towards the castle town of Saigadake. He then sought to re-engage the Takeda in person, to free his trapped general Mizuno Tadashige, but his retainer Natsume Yoshinobu persuaded him to retreat. Convinced that as head of the clan his life was too important, Ieyasu fled the field. To buy time, Natsume Yoshinobu led the few soldiers left under his command in a suicide charge against the advancing Takeda and was killed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Other notable Tokugawa retainers killed in the fighting were Matsudaira Koretada, Naruse Masayoshi, Toyama Kosaku and Endo Ukon, whose units were encircled and overrun by the Takeda forces. The Tokugawa and Oda force was almost totally destroyed. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamamatsu">Hamamatsu</a> Castle he was accompanied by only five men, and the town was on the verge of panic, as rumour had already reached it that the battle had gone badly.</p><p>Ieyasu commanded that the castle gates remain open and that braziers be lit to guide his retreating army to safety, and Sakai Tadatsugu beat a large war drum to persuade the returning men of their courageous retreat. The Takeda vanguard under Baba Nobuharu and Yamagata Masakage heard the drums and saw the braziers and open gates. They assumed that Ieyasu had planned a trap and stopped to make camp for the night where they stood, short of Hamamatsu.</p><p>In the night a small band of about 100 Tokugawa foot soldiers and 16 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matchlock">matchlock</a> gunners, led by Ōkubo Tadayo and Amano Yasukage, attacked the Takeda camp and threw the vanguard into confusion. Uncertain of the remaining strength of the Tokugawa, and worried that reinforcements from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-kenshin">Uesugi Kenshin</a> were on their way, Shingen decided to withdraw to his own territories. The skirmish took place near Saigagake, a narrow gorge about ten metres deep and 450 metres long, about one kilometre north of Hamamatsu Castle. There was to be no following attack on Hamamatsu: Shingen was fatally wounded in February 1573 at the Siege of Noda Castle and died in May 1573.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kōyō Gunkan, a record of the Takeda family's military exploits, is the source for the Takeda order of battle and for the claim that Shingen outnumbered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> three to one and drew up his army in the gyorin formation. It is a record kept by the Takeda family, the winning side.</p><p>Some of the best-known episodes of the night after the battle come from later sources. The Kansei Chōshū Shokafu, a genealogy of the major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> families compiled by the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a>, records that the Iga ninja Hattori Hanzō rendered meritorious service at Mikatagahara: he captured a Takeda spy named Chikuan and delayed the advancing Takeda at the Tenryū River with a counterattack of only thirty men.</p><p>The story of the Battle of Saigagake, as it is known in Japanese sources, is likewise an anecdote. In it the Tokugawa stretched a cloth across the cliff to look like a bridge, and the Takeda suffered numerous casualties trying to flee across it. This account appears in later records compiled by the Tokugawa shogunate and not in contemporary historical records, although the site of the alleged ruse was designated a Shizuoka Prefecture Historic Site in 1952.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen.jpg" alt="Takeda Shingen" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Takeda_Harunobu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Battle of Mikatagahara was one of the most famous battles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a>'s campaigns and one of the most notable demonstrations of cavalry tactics of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a>. It was also <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>'s most decisive defeat: his army was effectively annihilated, and he himself escaped death only narrowly, through a bluff and a perilous night attack.</p><p>The battle's effect on the campaign was limited by Shingen's own fate. His march towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, which had begun in October 1572 as a campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>, did not continue. He was fatally wounded in February 1573 at the Siege of Noda Castle and died in May 1573, so there was no following attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamamatsu">Hamamatsu</a>.</p><p>The episode of the open gates, the braziers and the war drum, and the tales of Natsume Yoshinobu's charge and of Saigagake, belong to the way the battle has been told. The site of the alleged Saigagake ruse is a Shizuoka Prefecture Historic Site, designated in 1952. The historian Kawai Atsushi's reading, that Ieyasu fought because he needed to keep the respect of the Mikawa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> clans, offers a reason why Ieyasu fought a far larger army against the advice of others.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>On 25 January 1573 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a>'s army, marching towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, overran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> and his Oda allies on the plain of Mikatagahara. Ieyasu escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamamatsu">Hamamatsu</a> with five men, and Shingen withdrew after a night attack; he died in May 1573.</em></p>

